# Code craft

Derived in part from Karpathy-inspired coding guidelines ([multica-ai/andrej-karpathy-skills](https://github.com/multica-ai/andrej-karpathy-skills), MIT). Merged into m4tr!x — do not install that repo wholesale.

For trivial patches, use judgment; do not run full ceremony.

## Decision hierarchy (when goals conflict)

1. Correctness, security, approved scope  
2. Existing project architecture and established patterns  
3. Production readiness and maintainability  
4. Appropriate reusability  
5. Simplicity and readability  
6. Token / execution efficiency  

## Think before coding

- Understand the task and inspect relevant existing code (components, helpers, tests, conventions) first.
- Surface material ambiguities, dependencies, and tradeoffs; do not silently pick among conflicting interpretations.
- Ask only when uncertainty affects correctness, security, scope, or important product behavior.
- Decide minor details yourself. Prefer the simplest appropriate approach; push back when a simpler fit exists.
- No lengthy analysis loops for straightforward tasks.

## Simplicity first

- Smallest **clear** implementation that fully meets the requirement — not code golf.
- No speculative features, premature generalization, or needless classes/interfaces/wrappers/services/files/deps.
- Prefer language/framework built-ins and existing project utilities.
- Preserve required validation, security, tests, and loading/error/empty behavior.
- If a senior engineer would call it overcomplicated, simplify before calling it done.
- Never cut lines at the expense of readability, correctness, or maintainability.

## Surgical changes

- Change only what the approved task requires; every meaningful edit should trace to that task.
- Preserve unrelated working behavior; match existing style; no drive-by refactors or formatting sweeps.
- Reuse existing components; do not create duplicates or shadow variants.
- Remove orphans **your** change created (unused imports/locals). Mention pre-existing debt — do not silently delete it.
- Small refactors only when essential for correct/clean implementation or justified reuse.

## Goal-driven execution

- Use clear, verifiable acceptance criteria sized to the task (patch ≠ module).
- Implement against those criteria; run appropriate checks; fix real failures before claiming done.
- Cover happy / fail / edge **proportionately**; report what was actually verified.
- Do **not** claim tests passed unless they were executed successfully.
